**Read:** [Spacecraft polymer atomic-oxygen durability — NASA-HDBK-6024](../sources/Atomic-Oxygen-Durability-NASA.lingua#r_1RMMZEYQQCZ08VRYTHCQBEEHJ0), interaction and measurement chapters, flight/ground comparisons and uncertainty appendices.

The environment must be specified even when the material and nominal mechanism are familiar. An atomic-oxygen ground facility can differ from orbital exposure in energy and arrival conditions. Witness-based effective fluence is a calibration quantity, not automatically the actual incident atom count. Moisture changes can obscure the small mass loss being attributed to erosion.

**Work:** Derive erosion yield from mass loss, exposed area, density and fluence, retaining dimensions. Reconstruct the conditioning and witness measurements and propagate their uncertainties, including shared-reference correlations. Compare erosion depth with mass-based inference. Explain how a coating defect can expose an underlying polymer and alter the damage geometry. Use this case to criticize laboratory-to-service transfer; it does not supply a complete account of ionizing radiation, displacement damage or reactor materials.

**Return:** [Materials engineering — structure, processing and useful behavior](../Materials-engineering.lingua#r_05BF5JM8141RKF1VT5998YFZQ9).
